      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Update: **In well lit worlds, the Quest Camera does not exhibit issues with saturation, contrast, and other balancing, and photos closely matches the camera display.**\nIssue appears to be reminiscent of older camera technology; in essence, the Photo Camera **lacks auto-exposure adjustment to account for dim lighting conditions, akin to that of a \"night mode\".**",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2022-03-27T21:05:46.096Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "# Update\n\n- **Issue occurs in PCVR; confirmed in version 1289.**\n\n- Behavior appears to be similar to the effect of increasing an image's \"nominal range\" by darkening dark colors and brightening bright colors. In dimly lit situations, this results in the majority of elements becoming \"washed out\" and muddy by the darkness, whilst bright elements become overly saturated and bright.\n\n- This also may contribute to why attempts to fix images \"in post\" result in brighter elements being washed out, as consumer editing tools will increase brightness uniformly, rather than on a curve.\n\nPhotos taken with the Photo Camera on Quest are currently fairly washed out. In particular:\n\n \n\n1. Colors are dull, and very dark. This can make images hard to see compared to how they look in-game.\n\n2. Avatar colors are darker still; This can make avatars look improperly lit compared to the environment.\n\n3. White/Black-point are seemingly off, along with brightness/contrast.\n\n \n\nCorrecting these in a simple photo editor results in better display, ***at the cost of emissive materials being washed out.***\n\n \n\n# Comparison photos attached:\n\n1. Unedited photo\n\n2. \"Balanced\" photo — Note locket is now white.\n\n3. Full saturation — Note eyes become bright and start to wash out.",
